Raskolnikov from Crime and Punishment is a particularly interesting example of what we’ve been discussing.

Even his name evokes raskol—a schism or split.

And that division runs through the entire character: intellect against conscience, ideology against experience, exceptionalism against ordinary human vulnerability.

What’s fascinating is that Raskolnikov tries to establish his own moral grammar through his theory of extraordinary individuals—people who may consider themselves entitled to transgress ordinary moral boundaries.

But his psychological experience doesn’t obediently conform to the rules he constructs.

Dostoevsky makes that conflict felt through the character’s feverish perceptions, the oppressive environment of St. Petersburg, his relationships, and the narrative itself.

The character isn’t merely divided. The reader experiences the division through the structure of the story.

And that connects back to your earlier idea about declaring authority: claiming a new position within a system doesn’t necessarily mean the rest of the system recognizes it.

